History records are per-id chains; cross-id record order doesn't matter (canonicalRecords sorts by id). So record ordering isn't a concern either way.

Decision: Add title uniqueness to `Workspace.add` (plain Error, `title already in use: <trimmed>`), and add an internal insertion path for the batch commit that defers title validation to the existing final-set check.

Implementation in workspace.js:

```js
add(input) {
  assertDocument(input);
  if (this.#documents.has(input.id)) throw new Error(`document already exists: ${input.id}`);
  const title = input.title.trim();
  for (const existing of this.#documents.values()) {
    if (existing.title === title) throw new Error(`title already in use: ${title}`);
  }
  const document = Object.freeze({ id: input.id, title, body: input.body, tags: normalizeTags(input.tags ?? []) });
  this.#documents.set(document.id, document);
  return structuredClone(document);
}
```

For the batch: I'll restructure commitBatch's mutate to apply removals before additions? Or add an unchecked method? Let me think about which is cleaner given the code style.

Actually, re-examine: with the removals-first approach, no new Workspace API is needed at all:

```js
const { snapshot, result } = commit((candidate, nextLedger) => {
  let changed = false;
  // Removals first: deletes, plus the ids replaced or restored. Titles freed
  // by the whole batch are therefore available to every addition, so a swap
  // or release-then-reuse never trips the per-add title check regardless of
  // operation order.
  for (const op of operations) {
    if (op.type === 'delete') candidate.remove(op.id);
    else if (op.type === 'replace' || op.type === 'restore') candidate.remove(op.type === 'replace' ? op.document.id : op.id);
  }
  for (const op of operations) {
    if (op.type === 'create') {
      candidate.add(op.document);  // may throw plain Error on genuine duplicate!
      ...
    }
  }
});
```

Problem: genuine final-state duplicates would surface as plain Error → 500 INTERNAL_ERROR instead of 409 "title conflict in batch". So I'd need to pre-validate titles on the projected final set before additions, or catch. Pre-validating before additions duplicates the final check; I could just do the title check on the projected set and keep adds after (they can't fail then). But computing the projected title set requires the normalized titles of added docs — `op.document.title.trim()` for create/replace, `target.document.title` (already normalized) for restore. That's doable:

After removals, before additions:
```js
const titles = new Map(); // title -> id
for (const doc of candidate.list()) titles.set(doc.title, doc.id);
for (const op of additions) {
  const title = (op.document ?? target.document).title.trim();
  if (titles.has(title)) throw new HttpError(409, 'CONFLICT', `title conflict in batch: ${title}`);
  titles.set(title, id);
}
```

Hmm wait, but the restore target document lookup: `ledger.entry(op.id, op.revision)` — available. This works but adds complexity and changes the error-message selection order slightly (which duplicate title gets reported when multiple). The existing final check iterates candidate.list() (sorted by id via localeCompare) and reports the first repeated title encountered. My pre-check iterates remaining docs then ops in op order. For a single-collision case, the reported title is the same (the duplicated title). Message identical: `title conflict in batch: <title>`. Good enough — but the README documents the message as `"title conflict in batch: …"`, fine.

Versus the `put` approach: keep everything exactly as-is, just swap `candidate.add` → `candidate.put` in commitBatch (3 spots), with the final check untouched. Minimal drift. But adds a public unchecked method to Workspace.

Hmm, actually there's a third option: keep `add` as the only mutator, and in commitBatch wrap the entire mutate in try/catch that converts a plain title Error into the 409. Combined with removals-first ordering, intermediate throws only happen for genuine final duplicates. The catch:

```js
try { ...ops... } catch (error) {
  if (error instanceof Error && error.message.startsWith('title already in use: ')) {
    throw new HttpError(409, 'CONFLICT', `title conflict in batch: ${error.message.slice(...)}`);
  }
  throw error;
}
```

That's stringly-typed and ugly.

I think the cleanest given the constraints is actually the removals-first + pre-check approach, OR just keep it simplest: give Workspace a `put` method. Let me look at how the final check message selection works now vs. alternatives once more.

Current commitBatch: applies ops in order (remove+add per op), then checks final set via candidate.list() order. If I keep that exact flow but with unchecked puts, behavior is 100% identical to today. That's the strongest guarantee for "保留批量修改已经支持的标题交换" and "批量结果确实重名时，继续整批返回现有的 409/CONFLICT".

I'll go with `put`. Naming: maybe `#insert` private + a public method? Since server.js is a separate module, it must be public. I'll document it clearly:

```js
